{"id":"rel.wave2.batch06.setoid-category.to.small-groupoid","from_formulation":"form.74cd6dc48e0606fc.setoid-category","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-groupoid","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"groupoid C","editorial_reason":"A setoid is a groupoid with no nonidentity automorphisms. The groupoid notion supplies the ambient invertible-arrow category, not the stronger setoid condition.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04S9","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0018","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_source_specific_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch06.field-extension.to.field-commutative-unital","from_formulation":"form.7210832bc589f307.field-extension","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.field-commutative-unital","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"fields","editorial_reason":"An extension E/F explicitly requires two fields; independently screened nonzero commutative unital field structure is a prerequisite to embedding F inside E.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FT","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FD","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_source_specific_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch06.field-characteristic.to.field-commutative-unital","from_formulation":"form.dbce785fa0c96b0a.field-characteristic","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.field-commutative-unital","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"field F","editorial_reason":"Characteristic is an invariant of a field defined by repeated addition of its multiplicative identity, so an underlying field is prerequisite.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FQ","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FD","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_source_specific_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch06.nth-root-of-unity.to.field-commutative-unital","from_formulation":"form.e0849711d61cd26f.nth-root-of-unity","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.field-commutative-unital","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"field F","editorial_reason":"An nth root of unity is a field element satisfying a multiplicative equation, which presupposes the field's multiplication and identity.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09HW","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FD","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_source_specific_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch06.multiplicative-monoid-character.to.field-commutative-unital","from_formulation":"form.0c2b2ef0c7e5c91b.multiplicative-monoid-character","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.field-commutative-unital","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"field L","editorial_reason":"A character valued in L uses the multiplicative monoid of L, requiring an underlying field even though the general monoid source and monoid homomorphism still need independent definitions.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CKK","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FD","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_source_specific_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch06.prime-field-finite.to.field-commutative-unital","from_formulation":"form.1fd51873f9f0afbd.prime-field-finite","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.field-commutative-unital","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"prime field","editorial_reason":"The finite prime-field construction requires the general field concept; the quotient ring structure and field property are separate, and no proof is implied by this relation.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FF","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FD","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_source_specific_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch06.finite-field.to.field-commutative-unital","from_formulation":"form.c8b6cfd6a058ab26.finite-field","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.field-commutative-unital","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"field","editorial_reason":"A finite field is an ordinary field with a finite underlying set. The new field axioms supply the base concept without entailing finiteness.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09HY","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FD","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_source_specific_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch06.strict-two-category.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.ec5e3a0d13f85f3a.strict-two-category","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"hom-categories","editorial_reason":"A strict 2-category has hom-categories with morphisms and associative composition. Ordinary small categories provide this foundational hom-level structure.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/003G","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_source_specific_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch06.field-commutative-unital.to.associative-ring-identity-optional","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.field-commutative-unital","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.associative-ring-identity-optional","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"commutative ring","editorial_reason":"A field is specified over a commutative unital ring. The independent associative-ring meaning is a valid structural prerequisite but does not alone impose commutativity, nonzero 1, or existence of inverses.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FD","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARing_%28Abstract_Algebra%29","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_source_specific_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch06.field-commutative-unital.to.unit-of-ring","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.field-commutative-unital","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.unit-of-ring","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"exists b∈k","editorial_reason":"The field condition demands inverses for every nonzero element; two-sided multiplicative units in unital rings supply that underlying inverse concept, while field nonzeroness is an extra requirement.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09FD","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AUnit_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_source_specific_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch06.category-functor.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.category-functor","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"small categories","editorial_reason":"The source's ordinary covariant functor is defined by mappings between the objects and arrows of small categories and preserves their compositions and identities. It presupposes the ordinary category axioms.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001B","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_source_specific_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
